---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Implementation Guide">
  <section class="py-16 bg-gradient-to-b from-secondary to-white dark:from-primary dark:to-primary/80">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-display font-bold mb-6">Implementation Guide</h1>
        <p class="text-xl mb-4 text-primary/80 dark:text-secondary/80">
          How to implement the Fair Witness Framework in your LLM applications
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  
  <section class="py-12">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert">
        <p>
          The Fair Witness Framework provides a structured approach to LLM interactions through YAML configuration. This guide walks through implementation steps for various LLM platforms.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Implementation Overview</h2>
        
        <p>
          Implementing the Fair Witness Framework involves three primary components:
        </p>
        
        <ol>
          <li>The YAML configuration defining epistemic functions and constraints</li>
          <li>The integration code for your chosen LLM platform</li>
          <li>The prompt template structure that leverages the framework</li>
        </ol>
        
        <p>
          This approach works with any LLM that accepts structured prompts or system instructions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  
  <section class="py-12 bg-secondary/30 dark:bg-primary/30">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold text-center mb-12">Quick Start Examples</h2>
      
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 max-w-5xl mx-auto">
        <div class="card">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-medium mb-4">OpenAI Integration</h3>
          
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-primary/50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4">
            <pre class="text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code class="language-javascript">import OpenAI from 'openai';
import yaml from 'js-yaml';
import fs from 'fs';

// Load Fair Witness configuration
const config = yaml.load(
  fs.readFileSync('./fair_witness_config.yaml', 'utf8')
);

const openai = new OpenAI({
  apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY,
});

async function fairWitnessQuery(prompt) {
  const systemPrompt = `You are a Fair Witness Bot operating 
under the following configuration:
${yaml.dump(config)}

Follow these constraints strictly when answering.`;

  const response = await openai.chat.completions.create({
    model: "gpt-4",
    messages: [
      { role: "system", content: systemPrompt },
      { role: "user", content: prompt }
    ],
    temperature: 0.7,
  });

  return response.choices[0].message.content;
}</code></pre>
          </div>
          
          <a href="/implementations/openai" class="text-accent1 hover:underline">Full OpenAI Implementation Guide →</a>
        </div>
        
        <div class="card">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-medium mb-4">LangChain Integration</h3>
          
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-primary/50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4">
            <pre class="text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code class="language-javascript">import { ChatOpenAI } from "langchain/chat_models/openai";
import { 
  ChatPromptTemplate, 
  SystemMessagePromptTemplate, 
  HumanMessagePromptTemplate 
} from "langchain/prompts";
import yaml from 'js-yaml';
import fs from 'fs';

// Load Fair Witness configuration
const config = yaml.load(
  fs.readFileSync('./fair_witness_config.yaml', 'utf8')
);

// Create system prompt with configuration
const systemTemplate = `You are a Fair Witness Bot operating 
under the following configuration:
${yaml.dump(config)}

Follow these constraints strictly when answering.`;

const chatPrompt = ChatPromptTemplate.fromPromptMessages([
  SystemMessagePromptTemplate.fromTemplate(systemTemplate),
  HumanMessagePromptTemplate.fromTemplate("{input}")
]);

const model = new ChatOpenAI({ temperature: 0.7 });

async function fairWitnessQuery(prompt) {
  const chain = chatPrompt.pipe(model);
  const response = await chain.invoke({
    input: prompt
  });
  return response.content;
}</code></pre>
          </div>
          
          <a href="/implementations/langchain" class="text-accent1 hover:underline">Full LangChain Implementation Guide →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  
  <section class="py-12">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold text-center mb-12">YAML Configuration Options</h2>
      
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <div class="card mb-8">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-medium mb-4">Core Configuration Structure</h3>
          
          <div class="bg-white dark:bg-primary/50 p-4 rounded-lg">
            <pre class="text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code class="language-yaml">emulation:
  type: Fair Witness Bot
  framework: Function-Epistemic Hybrid Framework
  epistemic_functions:
    - observer    # Required for objective data collection
    - evaluator   # Optional, for assessment against criteria
    - analyst     # Optional, for pattern identification
    - synthesist  # Optional, for integrating perspectives
    - communicator # Required for output formatting
  constraints:
    # RFC 2119 compliance for requirements language
    rfc: 2119
    # Optional code generation settings
    code_generation:
      principles: SOLID
      practices: Clean Code
      patterns: Pragmatic
    # Optional natural language settings
    natural_language:
      style: E-Prime  # Avoids forms of "to be" verbs
  output:
    type: natural language
    detail_level: range(moderate,high)
    length: range(moderate, high)
    complexity: range(low, high)
    style: dry</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="space-y-8">
          <div class="card">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-medium mb-4">Customizing Epistemic Functions</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">
              You can select which epistemic functions to include based on your use case. At minimum, including the observer and communicator functions provides the core Fair Witness functionality.
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-primary/80 dark:text-secondary/80">
              <li><strong>Analysis-focused:</strong> observer, analyst, communicator</li>
              <li><strong>Evaluation-focused:</strong> observer, evaluator, communicator</li>
              <li><strong>Comprehensive:</strong> all five functions</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          
          <div class="card">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-medium mb-4">Output Configuration</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">
              The output section controls the style and structure of LLM responses:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-primary/80 dark:text-secondary/80">
              <li><strong>detail_level:</strong> Controls information density (low, moderate, high)</li>
              <li><strong>length:</strong> Controls response length (short, moderate, high)</li>
              <li><strong>complexity:</strong> Controls language complexity (low, moderate, high)</li>
              <li><strong>style:</strong> Controls tone (dry, conversational, academic)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          
          <div class="card">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-medium mb-4">System Questions</h3>
            <p class="mb-4">
              The system_questions configuration enables structured question formats with identifiable slug references:
            </p>
            <pre class="text-sm bg-white dark:bg-primary/50 p-4 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto"><code class="language-yaml">output:
  system_questions:
    choices:
      - format: numbered list
      - number_of_questions: range(3,7)
      - aliases:
          required: true
          format: slug
          position: suffix</code></pre>
            <p class="mt-4">
              This produces questions with unique identifiers for programmatic reference:
            </p>
            <div class="bg-white dark:bg-primary/50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-4">
              <p class="italic">1. Which epistemic functions would benefit your specific use case most? <span class="text-accent1">_epistemic-priorities-slug_</span></p>
              <p class="italic">2. What evaluation criteria would you prioritize for your domain? <span class="text-accent1">_evaluation-criteria-slug_</span></p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="mt-8 flex justify-center">
          <a href="/yaml-configuration" class="btn btn-primary">Complete YAML Reference</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  
  <section class="py-12 bg-secondary/30 dark:bg-primary/30">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mb-6">Ready to Try It?</h2>
        <p class="text-xl mb-8 text-primary/80 dark:text-secondary/80">
          Get started with our quick implementation examples
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="/quick-start" class="btn btn-primary">Quick Start Guide</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/yourusername/fairwitnessbot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn btn-secondary">GitHub Repository</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
